# Decision documentation

Created: 2026-11-04T21:45:23Z (first recorded addition in Git)
Last updated: 2026-11-10T15:55:25Z

## When to write an ADR

Record consequential decisions affecting architecture, public contracts,
data correctness, security, or ongoing maintenance when their reasoning would
otherwise be difficult to reconstruct.

Routine implementation choices belong in the existing task record or relevant
code documentation. An ADR is not required for every code change.

## Location and numbering

Use one Markdown file per decision under `docs/decisions/`.

Continue the existing sequence using filenames such as
`0002-short-decision-title.md`. Use at least four digits, expanding naturally
when needed. Do not renumber existing records.

Link to research, task records, code, and related decisions rather than copying
their contents. Follow [documentation_core.mdc](documentation_core.mdc) for
audience, freshness metadata, and authoritative locations.

## Content

Keep each record proportional to the decision and include:

- Status.
- Context and the problem being addressed.
- Decision and rationale.
- Meaningful alternatives considered.
- Assumptions: what is believed or relied upon at the time.
- Invariants: conditions the implementation must preserve.
- Consequences, risks, and known uncertainties.
- Conditions that would justify revisiting the decision.
- Relevant repository commit references.

Distinguish observed facts from assumptions and unresolved questions.
Make assumptions and invariants specific enough to assess later.
If none are relevant, say so briefly rather than inventing content.

Record what was known when the decision was made. Link later findings without
rewriting earlier uncertainty as though it had already been resolved.

## Commit references

Include a full commit hash identifying the repository revision inspected when
forming the decision. Label it as the context revision.

If relevant work is uncommitted, identify that limitation; the context hash
does not describe uncommitted changes.

Add implementation commit references when available, identifying their role.
Until then, explicitly mark implementation references as pending or not applicable.

Do not invent hashes or imply that the context revision contains a later
implementation. An ADR cannot embed the hash of the commit that first adds
that same version of the document.

Recording commit references does not authorize creating commits.

## Status and acceptance

Use explicit states: Proposed, Accepted, Rejected, or Superseded.

Human agreement establishes acceptance. An accepted direction does not establish
that implementation, verification, or human review is complete.

Preserve rejected proposals when their reasoning remains useful.

## Changes and supersession

When an accepted decision materially changes, create a new ADR that explains
the new context and supersedes the earlier decision. Link both records and
mark the earlier record Superseded.

Permit factual corrections and clearly identified clarifications without
silently replacing the original decision or rationale.

Record substantive amendments so readers can distinguish the original context
from later information. Apply freshness rules from
[documentation_core.mdc](documentation_core.mdc).

## Related rules

Follow [research.mdc](research.mdc) for evidence and uncertainty, and
[coauthoring_protocol.mdc](coauthoring_protocol.mdc) for ownership and scope.

Commit-message and changelog practices belong in
[documentation_commits.mdc](documentation_commits.mdc) and
[changelog.mdc](changelog.mdc).
